Z||000007||JAMIE||01-24-2001||12:09 PM||||Probably the closest match you will find is Auto Custom Carpet - they sell carpet made from the original GM salt and pepper material and moulded to fit the car. You will also need to buy an extra yard for the doors. There are 2 things different from the oem carpet - no spongy sound deadening material under the carpet (just a sort of felt lining) and it has only 1 "heel pad" which is not same as original and is stitched on rather than heat moulded (original has 4 pads, 3 on driver side and 1 on passenger side.) I gave up looking for perfect new condition oem carpet and "made" a new set by starting with the ACC carpet,removing its felt backing and heel pad,removing the correct original backing from my old carpets and gluing it to the ACC carpet, and then removing the heel pads from my old carpet and carefully gluing them to the new carpet (this is the hardest part and takes a lot of time to do correctly - the trick is to mark out the locations of the pads on the new carpet and cut away the fibres to leave a bald flat surface to which the pads can be glued and which makes their edges flush with the surrounding fibres and looks identical to heat moulded originals.<BR>By doing this you can achieve perfect new carpet that is exactly the same as the original in every respect except one - the pads are glued and not heat moulded - the only way to tell the difference would be to remove the carpet from the car and look between the sound deadening liner and the underside of the carpet where you would see the heat moulding lines are missing.<P>Alot of work but you can get the stock correct rersult if that is important to you - to me it was essential as I am doing a showroom restoration.You could just buy the ACC stuff and install as is if a perfect match is not a concern.<P>Jamie<BR>||||reg||1
